566 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The most common attack type here is prompt injection, where the model is tricked into performing actions outside the original intent. However, there are other threats as well, including model reconnaissance and stealthy bias injection, which can be harder to detect.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
567 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (In response, the industry is focused on two key areas:)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
568 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 395 0 R
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
569 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (This has given rise to a niche group of vendors performing security testing for AI models, similar to how DAST tools scan web applications. These tools probe models for bias, robustness, and attack resistance before deployment.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
570 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Shockingly, this has also revived a lightweight form of RASP-style protection for AI applications, with runtime monitoring agents guarding against real-time threats.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
571 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (AI Security Market Report)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
