666 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Many vendors also provide a network proxy, which routes user requests to approved AI models. This proxy serves as a front end for monitoring model usage and detecting risky prompts. In many cases, this architecture also enables runtime application protection, since network requests can be intercepted and inspected in real time.)
/Pg 26 0 R
>>
endobj
667 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (For runtime application protection, most providers offer APIs as well as SDKs, which function as custom wrappers around those APIs, that developers can integrate into their applications. These SDKs help monitor for prompt injection and other attack types, while also enabling testing functionality before deploying AI capabilities in production.)
/Pg 26 0 R
>>
endobj
668 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Finally, third-party integrations are a common feature for both data loss prevention and posture management. )
/Pg 26 0 R
>>
endobj
